supporter: You are correct. If you want to create the German flag using CSS that is consistent with the official version of the flag, you need to use the correct colors and proportions. The official German flag is defined as having three stripes of equal size, with black on top, red in the middle, and golden yellow at the bottom. Additionally, the width-to-length ratio of the flag is 3:5. 

Here's an updated example implementation that should conform to these standards:

HTML:
```
<div class="flag-germany">
  <div class="flag-stripe flag-black"></div>
  <div class="flag-stripe flag-red"></div>
  <div class="flag-stripe flag-golden-yellow"></div>
</div>
```

CSS:
```
.flag-germany {
  width: 300px;
  height: 180px;
  display: flex;
}

.flag-stripe {
  flex: 1;
}

.flag-black {
  background-color: #000000;
}

.flag-red {
  background-color: #FF0000;
}

.flag-golden-yellow {
  background-color: #FFCE00;
}
```

This implementation uses a width-to-length ratio of 3:5, as specified in the official flag design. It also uses the official colors for the flag, which are black (#000000) for the top stripe, red (#FF0000) for the middle stripe, and golden yellow (#FFCE00) for the bottom stripe. The CSS `flex` property is used to create equal-width stripes, and the `display:flex;` property is applied to the `flag-germany` class so that the three div containers align horizontally. 

This implementation should allow you to create a German flag using CSS that is consistent with the official specifications of the flag.
